ew cooperation, communication, responsibility, adaptability.\n\nWe have no explicit stress resilience for A. B has stress handling. A's leadership and anticipation may suggest good decision making but not proven.\n\nWe could argue that while B is strong, A's ability to anticipate dangerous situations and see complex connections could be crucial for long-distance flights, and his leadership may offset some concerns, but lack of reliability evidence remains a gap. Perhaps we can suggest we need more info before final recommendation, but we must state a vote. We could keep vote for B, but note that A's strengths are notable but insufficient due to missing reliability and crew cooperation.\n\nThus we can say we still recommend B, but we need to explore other candidates (C, D) before final. So vote B.\n\nLet's craft message: agree with James that B remains leading, but highlight A's strengths and note missing evidence, and propose we still need to discuss C and D.\n\nWe must include explicit facts: A anticipates dangerous situations, sees complex connections, excellent spatial vision, very good leadership; also not good at taking criticism, unorganized. B is very conscientious, handles stress well, good at assessing weather, excellent computer skills, pretentious, wrong tone.\n\nWe can argue that B's conscientiousness directly addresses reliability, while A lacks that evidence. So we keep B.\n\nThus public message: \"I agree with James that B remains the strongest candidate given the concrete reliability and stress evidence, while A's strengths are promising but insufficient without reliability/crew cooperation data.\"\n\nVote B."}
